# The CLF suite (docs/impl/ocio.md §7.3). Every row's expected output is
# PUBLISHED — either the specification's own worked example, a formula the file
# itself states, or arithmetic on the file's own published numbers. Nothing here
# was produced by lumit-colour, and nothing here needs the reference library.
#
# The `.clf` files beside this one are verbatim, byte-for-byte, as the
# OpenColorIO project distributes the Common LUT Format specification's example
# and implementation-test documents (BSD-3-Clause; the documents themselves are
# the Academy/SMPTE ST 2136-1 examples). They are not edited — a vendored file
# that someone tidied is no longer the published file — so their provenance is
# stated here rather than inside them.
#
# PROVENANCE AND DERIVATION, PER FILE
#
#   matrix_3x4_example.clf
#     Spec example `exmat1` ("transform example matrix"). A 3×4 matrix at
#     10i in, 12i out. Expected = M·rgb × (1023/4095) + offset/4095, the
#     bit-depth scaling the specification defines. Exact arithmetic on the
#     file's own coefficients.
#
#   lut1d_example.clf
#     Spec example `exlut1`. 65 entries, 8i in, 12i out, and the file states
#     its own generating formula in a Description: `flipud(1.25 - 1.5*x^2.2)`.
#     Expected = that formula at x = 1 − t (the flip), which is why entry 0 is
#     −1023.75 (= −0.25 × 4095) and entry 64 is 5118.75 (= 1.25 × 4095). The
#     published formula and the published table agree to eight decimals, so the
#     row gates the reader rather than the table.
#
#   lut3d_identity_12i_16f.clf
#     Spec example `exlut2`. A 2³ cube that is the identity — published by
#     construction, and the sharpest possible test of CLF's blue-fastest
#     storage order: read in file order the red and blue axes swap, and the
#     identity becomes a transpose that still looks like a plausible grade.
#
#   tabulation_support.clf
#     The implementation-test file for tab separators. A 3³ cube at 10i/10i
#     whose samples deliberately run outside 0–1023. Expected at a grid node =
#     that node's own published triple ÷ 1023 — a look-up at an exact grid
#     point returns the stored sample, so no interpolation enters the row. The
#     three rows are chosen at three different corners, so a transposed axis
#     cannot pass.
#
#   range.clf
#     The implementation-test file for a Range with no `style` attribute: the
#     specification's default is to clamp, and the file's own Description says
#     so. 16i in and out with equal in and out bounds, i.e. identity inside
#     [16320, 32640]/65535 and a clamp outside it.
#
#   range_test1_noclamp.clf
#     The same node with `style="noClamp"`, 8i in and 32f out, and bounds
#     deliberately outside the 8-bit range. Expected = the affine map the
#     specification defines, (x − minIn)·(maxOut − minOut)/(maxIn − minIn) +
#     minOut, evaluated past both ends to prove nothing clamps.
#
#   difficult_syntax.clf
#     The specification's syntax torture document: XML comments inside an
#     Array and inside a Description, entity escapes, tabs as separators,
#     multi-line attribute values, `+` signs and exponent notation, an unknown
#     attribute, an `Info` block with vendor elements. Its maths is the
#     published `xyz_to_rgb` example — the XYZ→sRGB matrix, a clamping Range,
#     then a 128×3 table the file describes as `x^1/1.8, with 0.95 and 0.9
#     scaling for G and B`. Both rows are chosen to clamp hard at an end of the
#     Range, so the expected value is a published table endpoint exactly and no
#     interpolation is involved: the row measures the *reading*, which is the
#     whole point of this file.
#
#   info_example.clf
#     The implementation-test file for the `Info` element, whose contents are
#     application-specific by definition and must be skipped whole. Its
#     transform is a 32f→12i matrix of 4095s, i.e. the identity once the output
#     bit depth comes out. Anything but the identity here means the bit-depth
#     scaling was dropped; a parse error means the vendor elements inside
#     `Info` were read as process nodes.
#
# Format: <clf file> | <r g b in> | <R G B expected> | <tolerance>

matrix_3x4_example.clf   | 1.0 0.0 0.0   | 0.89941392 0.04995116 0.02488400   | 1e-6
matrix_3x4_example.clf   | 0.0 1.0 0.0   | 0.02505495 0.87434676 -0.07504274  | 1e-6
matrix_3x4_example.clf   | 0.2 0.5 0.8   | 0.15246154 0.46714530 0.64692796   | 1e-6
# The offsets alone, which is where a writer that forgot to divide them by the
# output depth is off by a factor of 4095 and nothing else moves.
matrix_3x4_example.clf   | 0.0 0.0 0.0   | 0.00007326 -0.00001221 -0.00009768 | 1e-7

